<section xml:id="sec-length-of-a-line-in-three-dimensions" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Length of a Line in Three Dimensions</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        2.0 Measurements and Geometry
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        2.4 Three Dimensional Geometry
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title> 
                    <p>
                        Calculate the length of a line and projection of line on a plane in a 3-D object
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        12
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <!-- Planning notes (from the Combined Mathematics tab of the Grade 12 Book Structure Mapping sheet)
         Content summary: Lengths of edges, face diagonals and space diagonals in solids are found by repeated use of Pythagoras' theorem, e.g. the space diagonal of a cuboid = √(l² + w² + h²).
         Suggested activity: Learners measure the length, width and height of a carton (or the classroom), calculate the space diagonal, then check it with a string stretched corner to corner.
         Interactive (JSXGraph): Cuboid with sliders for length, width and height; the face diagonal and space diagonal are drawn and their lengths calculated, with the two right-angled triangles shaded. -->

